I had enjoyed visiting the local theatre for years, but I knew little about what happened before an audience arrived. When the theatre advertised a day for volunteers, I signed up. {{1}}

At nine, the stage manager introduced the team and explained the schedule. We would first check the set, then help prepare the foyer. {{2}}

I expected the set to be a collection of heavy walls. Instead, several pieces were light enough for two people to move. A technician showed us the marks on the floor that kept each item in exactly the right place. {{3}}

After lunch, I worked with the front-of-house team. They explained how to guide visitors, answer questions and respond calmly when a ticket could not be found. {{4}}

The performance began at seven. From a corner of the foyer, I watched families and friends arrive, each unaware of the careful preparation behind the evening. {{5}}

At the end, the stage manager asked what had surprised us most. I said it was not a particular piece of equipment but the constant exchange of information. {{6}}

The director of a regional museum began with a question that sounded straightforward: who was not visiting? Attendance figures showed that some districts were barely represented. {{1}}

The team first changed opening hours, assuming that work schedules were the main barrier. Evening visits increased, but the overall pattern remained. {{2}}

Staff then invited several local groups to comment on a planned exhibition. The first meetings were uncomfortable; participants questioned not only the display but the language the museum used to describe the region. {{3}}

Rather than ask visitors to approve a finished plan, the museum created a small paid advisory group. Its members could challenge choices while there was still time to change them. {{4}}

One resulting display focused on everyday work in the area, combining objects from the collection with recent personal accounts. It drew new visitors, but the director resisted presenting this as proof that every problem had been solved. {{5}}

The museum now publishes attendance by district alongside comments about the visitor experience. That information can be awkward to read, yet it helps staff decide what to change next. {{6}}
